@andrewsykim Are there any reasons to set net.ipv4.conf.all.route_localnet=1 in the ipvs proxier?

If not, I will make a PR to remove it.

Considering the problems described in #67730 I don't think it makes any difference.

> @andrewsykim Are there any reasons to set net.ipv4.conf.all.route_localnet=1 in the ipvs proxier?

@uablrek I don't have the full context for this but in doing some digging I think this was mostly added because iptables had it. I wouldn't be surprised though if disabling it broke some functionality that users depend on. 

For IPVS, even though we don't permit node port via localhost (yet), it sounds like it still has implications for when the source is from localhost?

> Does anyone know about out-of-tree implementations? What do they support?

@thockin Cilium's BPF-based kube-proxy replacement supports accessing a NodePort service via `127.0.0.1:$NODE_PORT`, and thanks to BPF it does NOT set `net.ipv4.conf.all.route_localnet=1` (therefore, it's not vulnerable to CVE-2020-8558). See https://docs.cilium.io/en/v1.8/gettingstarted/kubeproxy-free/#nodeport-devices-port-and-bind-settings:

> the NodePort service can be accessed by default from a host or a pod within a cluster via its public, any local (except for docker* prefixed names) or loopback address, e.g. 127.0.0.1:NODE_PORT.

@uablrek You probably ran on an old kernel version which does not support some features required by the BPF NodePort. You can check `cilium status` to see what the kube-proxy replacement features were enabled.
